Yes, indeed. That's an universally true statement (for all my games);
I still haven't found "the one way" to always "solve" the game. ;-)

But it also depends on what you _plan_ to do. An example; say I cannot
enter the castle (I have no levitation, no water walking, no scroll of
earth or boulder and no wand of striking, etc. etc.) then I might want
to go for a music instrument... - but is it safe to enter the barracks
without reflection? The typical answer is no. But if I cannot proceed
further in the game a source of reflection might be more advantageous
than, say, a GDSM at that stage of the game in case you've got to get
a random wish.

> and how many wishes you have available. [...]

Yes.

> By the time I get the castle wand, I
> rarely need all of the wishes it gives me, and if I don't have
> reflection by then, it's definitely worth a wish. 

Well, it depends. As a player typically not equipped with "everything"
I have to make decisions; When to wish for What. So my playing style
is to delay wishes as long as possible, i.e. as long as I can feel[*]
safe; some lookahead and quite some experience is certainly necessary
for that.

[*] You may be pondering about the word "feel" that I used; actually
it implies use of player intuition based on the actual game state and
some knowledge of probabilities (or just experience) within the game.

